        "<p>This mechanical boundary condition establishes that the photospheric pressure is directly proportional to the surface gravity \\( g(R) \\) and inversely proportional to the Rosseland mean opacity \\( \\kappa(R) \\). In the limiting case of a perfectly transparent stellar atmosphere where the opacity \\( \\kappa(R) \\) approaches zero, the optical depth vanishes and the photospheric pressure \\( P(R) \\) diverges mathematically, meaning that the boundary between the star and empty space contracts into a sharp, discontinuous interface. Under this zero-opacity limit, the gas remains in perfect thermodynamic isolation, and the non-linear atmospheric equations contract into simple vacuum equations where the pressure drops abruptly to zero at the stellar radius. This transition mathematically guarantees that the classical sharp-boundary limit is recovered, allowing stellar structure calculations to proceed without modeling the complex radiative transfer of the stellar wind. Under this continuous reduction, the non-linear atmospheric boundaries contract into simple classical limits that govern static laboratory vacuum thermal systems.</p>"
